    "lawCode": "Amd §§1 & 3, Chap 264 of 1981; amd §97-www, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1753\n\nSPONSOR: BORRELLO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 264 of the laws of 1981, relating to authorizing\nthe sale of certain lands in the towns of Cuba, Allegany county and\nIschua, Cattaraugus county, and the state finance law, in relation to\nthe sale of certain lands in the towns of Cuba, Allegany county and\nIschua, Cattaraugus county, and the disbursement of monies from the Cuba\nlake management fund\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRelates to the sale of certain lands in the towns of Cuba, Allegany\nCounty and Ischua, Cattaraugus County, and the disbursement of monies\nfrom the Cuba lake management fund.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Sections 1 and 3 of chapter 264 of the laws of 1981 relating\nto the sale of certain lands in the towns of Cuba, Allegany county and\nIschua, Cattaraugus county, as amended by chapter 644 of the laws of\n1983 are amended to sell that lands at a reasonable value.\n\nSection 2: Subdivisions 2, 3, and 6 of section 97-www of the state\nfinance law as added by chapter 342 of the laws of 2000, are amended to\ninclude the maintenance of the spillway and dam by the state after such\nsale of the property.\n\nSection 3: Effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCuba Lake is a human-made lake located partially in Cattaraugus County\nand in Allegany County. The Lake was created by the State of New York in\nthe middle century to supply water to the Genesee Valley Canal.  Once\nthe State discontinued the Genesee Valley Canal, over decades, the lands\naround the Lake were leased by the State to private individuals in small\nlots for recreational use. By the mid-1970s, cottages around the Lake\nhad become significantly improved; and the waters of the Lake had become\na source of substantial public recreation. In 1981, the cottage owners\nat Cuba Lake and the Office of General Services concluded that it would\nbe to the advantage of the State and the cottage owners that the State\nestablish a special use district to manage the Lake. The legislation\ncreating the Cuba Lake District drafted in broad powers to permit full\nDistrict management the Lake and, as provided by companion legislation,\nenvisioning the District's acquisition of the land around the Lake as\npart of its administration. (See Chapters 263 and 264 of the Laws of\n1981)\n\nShortly after the establishment of the District and completion of\nrelated actions to bring it into existence, dialogue began between the\nDistrict and OGS for terms of a possible purchase of the lake lands by\nthe District. Later, in the 1990s, after years of negotiation, an agree-\nment was reached, but was not finalized due to the award of a portion of\nthe Cuba Lake to the Seneca Nation of Indian lands.\n\n\nThis bill will finalize the sale of the land to from the State to the\nCuba Lake management fund, with the State maintaining the spillway and\ndam.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: S.8812/A.10945 Referred to Rules\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.  For the sale\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
